1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,192
Hi everyone, and welcome to Linear Algebra at WenQuest.

2
00:00:04,592 --> 00:00:08,029
This is Chapter 14, Section 2, the spectral theorem.

3
00:00:09,549 --> 00:00:12,060
Let's start with a problem from the shop floor.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:15,711
A robot arm holds a grinding wheel, or a deburring tool.

5
00:00:15,731 --> 00:00:18,690
The tool presses on the part, the part pushes back,

6
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:21,144
and the tip of the arm gives way a little.

7
00:00:21,544 --> 00:00:25,706
That little bit can be a few tenths of a millimetre of error on the finished part.

8
00:00:26,106 --> 00:00:29,984
So an engineer wants to know: in which direction is the tip softest,

9
00:00:30,004 --> 00:00:33,138
in which is it stiffest, and how big is the difference?

10
00:00:33,538 --> 00:00:37,680
And a more practical question: if you push in some arbitrary direction,

11
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:39,498
where does the tip actually go?

12
00:00:39,898 --> 00:00:44,147
In the last section we proved that the tip stiffness matrix is symmetric,

13
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:48,006
and that the eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix are perpendicular.

14
00:00:48,406 --> 00:00:50,907
If we can always find a full set of them,

15
00:00:50,927 --> 00:00:55,455
the stiffness matrix is just a few independent springs along perpendicular

16
00:00:55,475 --> 00:00:56,132
directions.

17
00:00:56,532 --> 00:01:00,070
Today's result, the spectral theorem, guarantees exactly that.

18
00:01:01,590 --> 00:01:06,490
The spectral theorem says: let A be a real symmetric matrix of size n by n.

19
00:01:06,890 --> 00:01:12,235
Then there is an orthogonal matrix Q and a real diagonal matrix Lambda such

20
00:01:12,255 --> 00:01:15,597
that A equals Q times Lambda times Q transpose.

21
00:01:15,997 --> 00:01:19,670
Orthogonal means Q transpose times Q is the identity.

22
00:01:19,690 --> 00:01:22,666
The columns are perpendicular unit vectors.

23
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:27,205
The columns of Q are an orthonormal set of eigenvectors of A,

24
00:01:27,225 --> 00:01:31,569
and the diagonal entries of Lambda are the matching eigenvalues.

25
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:33,639
The converse is true too.

26
00:01:33,659 --> 00:01:38,301
Any matrix of the form Q Lambda Q transpose equals its own transpose,

27
00:01:38,321 --> 00:01:39,585
so it is symmetric.

28
00:01:39,985 --> 00:01:40,902
In other words,

29
00:01:40,922 --> 00:01:45,710
being real symmetric and being diagonalizable by an orthogonal matrix are the

30
00:01:45,730 --> 00:01:46,397
same thing.

31
00:01:47,917 --> 00:01:51,544
The proof is by induction. Let's look only at the key step.

32
00:01:51,944 --> 00:01:57,599
The characteristic polynomial always has a root, so take an eigenvalue λ₁ of A.

33
00:01:57,619 --> 00:02:02,555
We showed last time it must be real. Take a real unit eigenvector q₁,

34
00:02:02,575 --> 00:02:04,997
extend it to an orthonormal basis,

35
00:02:05,017 --> 00:02:08,373
and put the basis into an orthogonal matrix Q₁.

36
00:02:08,773 --> 00:02:12,164
Now change coordinates with Q₁ and see what A becomes.

37
00:02:12,564 --> 00:02:16,926
The lower-left block is UᵀAq₁, which is λ₁Uᵀq₁.

38
00:02:16,946 --> 00:02:22,892
The columns of U are perpendicular to q₁, so this block is zero.

39
00:02:23,292 --> 00:02:24,580
Here is the key point.

40
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:28,325
The upper-right block is the transpose of the lower-left block,

41
00:02:28,345 --> 00:02:29,633
so it is zero as well.

42
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:33,715
For a general matrix this step clears only the first column.

43
00:02:33,735 --> 00:02:37,232
For a symmetric matrix the first row is cleared for free.

44
00:02:37,632 --> 00:02:41,231
What is left, B, is still symmetric and one size smaller.

45
00:02:41,251 --> 00:02:44,786
Repeat the same step on B, peel off one layer at a time,

46
00:02:44,806 --> 00:02:47,008
and you end with a diagonal matrix.

47
00:02:48,528 --> 00:02:51,024
The theorem has some immediate consequences.

48
00:02:51,424 --> 00:02:54,894
First, a symmetric matrix can always be diagonalized.

49
00:02:54,914 --> 00:02:59,765
Every eigenvalue has as many independent eigenvectors as its multiplicity,

50
00:02:59,785 --> 00:03:04,176
so unlike the shear matrix in the last chapter, we never run short.

51
00:03:04,576 --> 00:03:07,533
Second, the diagonalizing matrix is orthogonal.

52
00:03:07,553 --> 00:03:11,649
Its inverse is just its transpose, so there is nothing to invert.

53
00:03:12,049 --> 00:03:14,330
You will often see this stated as:

54
00:03:14,350 --> 00:03:19,271
every real symmetric matrix is orthogonally similar to a diagonal matrix.

55
00:03:19,671 --> 00:03:23,351
In code, always use numpy's eigh for symmetric matrices.

56
00:03:23,371 --> 00:03:28,108
It returns the eigenvalues in increasing order and an orthonormal set of

57
00:03:28,128 --> 00:03:28,966
eigenvectors.

58
00:03:30,486 --> 00:03:35,537
So what does A = QΛQᵀ mean geometrically? Let's watch.

59
00:03:35,937 --> 00:03:41,344
Here A is the 2 × 2 symmetric matrix (2 1; 1 2). Its eigenvalues are 1 and 3,

60
00:03:41,364 --> 00:03:45,079
and the green and red eigenvectors are perpendicular.

61
00:03:45,479 --> 00:03:47,501
Step one. Qᵀ is a rotation.

62
00:03:47,521 --> 00:03:51,660
It turns the two eigenvectors onto the coordinate axes.

63
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,685
Q can always be chosen to be a rotation.

64
00:03:55,085 --> 00:03:57,713
Step two. Λ stretches along the axes,

65
00:03:57,733 --> 00:04:01,076
by a factor of 1 horizontally and 3 vertically.

66
00:04:01,096 --> 00:04:03,581
The two directions don't interfere.

67
00:04:03,981 --> 00:04:05,807
Step three. Q rotates back.

68
00:04:05,827 --> 00:04:11,005
The unit circle has become an ellipse whose axes lie along the eigenvectors.

69
00:04:11,405 --> 00:04:14,122
Finally, transform the circle by A directly.

70
00:04:14,142 --> 00:04:18,040
The white curve lands exactly on the result of the three steps.

71
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:19,453
In one sentence:

72
00:04:19,473 --> 00:04:24,617
a symmetric matrix stretches space independently along perpendicular directions.

73
00:04:24,637 --> 00:04:29,523
The circle becomes an ellipse whose semi-axes are the absolute values of the

74
00:04:29,543 --> 00:04:30,297
eigenvalues.

75
00:04:31,817 --> 00:04:37,953
Multiply out QΛQᵀ as columns times rows, and you get another way to write it.

76
00:04:38,353 --> 00:04:46,979
A = λ₁q₁q₁ᵀ + λ₂q₂q₂ᵀ + … + λₙqₙqₙᵀ.

77
00:04:47,379 --> 00:04:51,024
Each qᵢqᵢᵀ is the projection onto the i-th axis.

78
00:04:51,044 --> 00:04:54,154
These projections add up to the identity,

79
00:04:54,174 --> 00:04:57,514
and any two different ones multiply to zero.

80
00:04:57,914 --> 00:05:02,284
So a symmetric matrix is a weighted sum of perpendicular projections,

81
00:05:02,304 --> 00:05:05,911
and the weights are the eigenvalues. Applied to a vector,

82
00:05:05,931 --> 00:05:10,619
it projects onto each axis, scales by the eigenvalue, and adds the pieces.

83
00:05:10,639 --> 00:05:12,846
This is the spectral decomposition.

84
00:05:13,246 --> 00:05:17,016
It makes functions of a matrix easy. To get f(A),

85
00:05:17,036 --> 00:05:20,032
just replace each eigenvalue λ by f(λ).

86
00:05:20,432 --> 00:05:25,200
For example, the compliant wrist has stiffness K equal to (4 1; 1 4),

87
00:05:25,220 --> 00:05:26,935
with eigenvalues 3 and 5.

88
00:05:26,955 --> 00:05:31,099
Replace them by √3 and √5 and you have the square root of K.

89
00:05:31,119 --> 00:05:36,095
Square roots of symmetric positive definite matrices show up in weighted

90
00:05:36,115 --> 00:05:38,593
least squares and in Kalman filters.

91
00:05:40,113 --> 00:05:47,222
Back to the robot. A planar two-link arm has links of 0.4 and 0.3 metres,

92
00:05:47,242 --> 00:05:51,812
and joint stiffnesses of 2000 and 1000 N·m/rad.

93
00:05:52,212 --> 00:05:58,114
The tip compliance is C = JK_q⁻¹Jᵀ, and the tip stiffness is its inverse.

94
00:05:58,134 --> 00:06:03,225
At the pose (0°, 90°), the program gives this stiffness matrix,

95
00:06:03,245 --> 00:06:04,848
and it is symmetric.

96
00:06:05,248 --> 00:06:11,187
Run eigh, and the principal stiffnesses come out as 5764 and 24097 N/m,

97
00:06:11,207 --> 00:06:16,559
more than four times apart. The soft axis points at 147 degrees,

98
00:06:16,579 --> 00:06:21,260
the stiff axis at 57 degrees, perpendicular as promised.

99
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:23,941
Now push straight down with 10 newtons.

100
00:06:23,961 --> 00:06:26,891
The spectral decomposition does it in three steps.

101
00:06:27,291 --> 00:06:30,798
Step one, split the force onto the two axes.

102
00:06:30,818 --> 00:06:36,089
About 5.4 newtons land on the soft axis and 8.4 on the stiff axis.

103
00:06:36,489 --> 00:06:39,707
Step two, divide each by its stiffness.

104
00:06:39,727 --> 00:06:45,436
The soft axis moves about 0.94 millimetres, the stiff axis only 0.35.

105
00:06:45,836 --> 00:06:51,150
Step three, add them back. The deflection is (0.6, −0.8) millimetres,

106
00:06:51,170 --> 00:06:54,243
the same as solving the system directly.

107
00:06:54,643 --> 00:06:57,059
Notice that the push is straight down,

108
00:06:57,079 --> 00:07:00,778
yet the deflection is about 37 degrees away from the push,

109
00:07:00,798 --> 00:07:02,125
toward the soft axis.

110
00:07:02,145 --> 00:07:06,421
Push with 10 newtons in every direction and the tips trace out this

111
00:07:06,441 --> 00:07:07,640
compliance ellipse.

112
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:12,173
All these numbers come from Program 14.2.1.

113
00:07:12,193 --> 00:07:15,701
Every number in the book is computed by a program.

114
00:07:16,101 --> 00:07:19,248
The heart of it is a few lines. Build the compliance,

115
00:07:19,268 --> 00:07:23,731
invert it to get the stiffness, call eigh, project the force onto the axes,

116
00:07:23,751 --> 00:07:26,301
divide by the stiffnesses, and rotate back.

117
00:07:26,701 --> 00:07:30,200
Here is the output. You can run it on the platform yourself,

118
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:32,488
change the pose or the joint stiffness,

119
00:07:32,508 --> 00:07:35,011
and watch the principal stiffnesses change.

120
00:07:36,531 --> 00:07:41,385
Now let's open Lab 14.2, the stiffness ellipse at a robot's tip.

121
00:07:41,785 --> 00:07:44,826
Here is a planar two-link arm with elastic joints.

122
00:07:44,846 --> 00:07:48,071
The ellipse around the tip is the compliance ellipse.

123
00:07:48,091 --> 00:07:52,234
Its long axis is the soft axis and its short axis is the stiff axis.

124
00:07:52,634 --> 00:07:55,812
Drag the push direction φ. Except along the two axes,

125
00:07:55,832 --> 00:08:00,700
the deflection always swerves toward the soft axis instead of following the push.

126
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:04,334
The readout on the right shows the angle between them.

127
00:08:04,354 --> 00:08:07,587
It is zero only when the push lies exactly on an axis.

128
00:08:07,607 --> 00:08:11,926
Your first task is to turn the push onto the softest direction yourself,

129
00:08:11,946 --> 00:08:16,023
so that the deflection lines up with it and is as large as possible.

130
00:08:16,423 --> 00:08:20,611
Now turn the first joint, and keep an eye on the two principal stiffnesses.

131
00:08:21,011 --> 00:08:25,614
The axes turn with the arm, but the stiffness values do not change. Why not?

132
00:08:25,634 --> 00:08:28,533
That is the question to think about in this lab.

133
00:08:28,933 --> 00:08:31,261
Now change the angle of the second joint.

134
00:08:31,281 --> 00:08:33,838
Make it smaller and the ellipse gets flatter.

135
00:08:34,238 --> 00:08:36,435
Make it larger and it gets rounder. So,

136
00:08:36,455 --> 00:08:40,187
can you make the tip equally stiff in every direction? Which pose,

137
00:08:40,207 --> 00:08:43,882
which stiffness? Each of the four tasks is checked automatically,

138
00:08:43,902 --> 00:08:47,066
and the lab guide and report template are ready for you.

139
00:08:48,586 --> 00:08:52,837
What you learned today is used in the WenQuest digital factory as well.

140
00:08:53,237 --> 00:08:55,954
This is the motion and dynamics module.

141
00:08:55,974 --> 00:09:00,517
It uses MuJoCo to simulate a UR5e arm carrying a 3 kilogram load,

142
00:09:00,537 --> 00:09:02,763
plots the torque at every joint,

143
00:09:02,783 --> 00:09:06,553
and then picks the motors and gearboxes automatically.

144
00:09:06,953 --> 00:09:08,427
A gearbox is not rigid.

145
00:09:08,447 --> 00:09:12,976
Its torsional stiffness is the main source of the joint stiffness k in

146
00:09:12,996 --> 00:09:14,016
today's formula.

147
00:09:14,416 --> 00:09:16,391
Once the gearbox is chosen,

148
00:09:16,411 --> 00:09:20,381
you can plug its stiffness into C = JK_q⁻¹Jᵀ yourself,

149
00:09:20,401 --> 00:09:24,371
get the arm's stiffness ellipsoid in its working pose,

150
00:09:24,391 --> 00:09:27,475
and see which way the tool will be pushed.

151
00:09:27,875 --> 00:09:33,697
On the platform you can try today's method on the digital factory's UR5e simulation.

152
00:09:35,217 --> 00:09:37,701
There are four exercises for this lecture.

153
00:09:38,101 --> 00:09:43,628
The first is a computation. Write a 2 × 2 symmetric matrix as QΛQᵀ,

154
00:09:43,648 --> 00:09:46,278
and as a spectral decomposition.

155
00:09:46,678 --> 00:09:51,133
The second is a proof. If a symmetric matrix satisfies A² = A,

156
00:09:51,153 --> 00:09:53,731
show it is an orthogonal projection.

157
00:09:54,131 --> 00:09:55,642
The third is programming.

158
00:09:55,662 --> 00:10:00,784
Compute a matrix exponential from the spectral decomposition and compare with scipy.

159
00:10:01,184 --> 00:10:02,993
The fourth is about the robot.

160
00:10:03,013 --> 00:10:06,407
Find the principal stiffnesses and axes at another pose,

161
00:10:06,427 --> 00:10:10,187
and explain why turning the first joint leaves them unchanged.

162
00:10:10,587 --> 00:10:12,505
Plus the report for Lab 14.2.

163
00:10:12,525 --> 00:10:17,450
Submit your work on the platform and the AI gives you feedback right away,

164
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:18,319
step by step.

165
00:10:19,839 --> 00:10:21,926
To sum up in three sentences.

166
00:10:21,946 --> 00:10:27,086
A real symmetric matrix is exactly a matrix you can diagonalize with an

167
00:10:27,106 --> 00:10:28,394
orthogonal matrix.

168
00:10:28,794 --> 00:10:32,790
Geometrically, it rotates, stretches along perpendicular axes,

169
00:10:32,810 --> 00:10:33,891
and rotates back.

170
00:10:34,291 --> 00:10:38,366
The spectral decomposition writes it as a weighted sum of projections,

171
00:10:38,386 --> 00:10:42,518
and the axes of a robot's stiffness ellipse are the eigenvectors of its

172
00:10:42,538 --> 00:10:43,513
stiffness matrix.

173
00:10:43,913 --> 00:10:45,335
The lab, the program,

174
00:10:45,355 --> 00:10:50,279
the notes and the exercises are all on the WenQuest learning platform at

175
00:10:50,299 --> 00:10:52,133
learn.wenquestrobotics.com.

176
00:10:52,153 --> 00:10:54,811
Sign up for free and try them yourself.

177
00:10:55,211 --> 00:10:59,153
Next time we extend the spectral theorem to complex matrices,

178
00:10:59,173 --> 00:11:04,220
with Hermitian and unitary matrices and the Schur decomposition. See you then.
